{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAozbBPRixugsH55wqxi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator, but the fix is untested on the watch: I didn't push anything to Intervals or Garmin. I only checked that `gen.py` now writes `start`/`end` for every HR step, with no `value` left.\n\n**The fix:** a one-word change in `hr_target()` in `gen.py`. HR targets are now `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` instead of `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. I also added a docstring explaining why, so nobody changes it back.\n\n**Why:** in your watch notes, every step lands exactly on one of the watch's zone bands. Changing the watch's LTHR moves the bands, but you never get the planned ranges. That fits the colleague's finding: Intervals sends `{value, end}` to the watch as a zone-based target, which the watch snaps to its own zones. `{start, end}` goes as an exact bpm range worked out from the LTHR stored in Intervals (172). Your two failed attempts were also the same as theirs (the 214–249 bpm range and the constant 101), which supports it.\n\n**To confirm:**\n1. Regenerate and push `plans/tempo-run.json`.\n2. Read the event back from the API and check that `start` survived and the steps aren't empty.\n3. Sync the watch and look at the workout.\n\nIt should show warm-up 117–129, tempo 151–160, float 129–141 and cool-down 112–124 bpm. Since the watch's LTHR is 168 and Intervals' is 172, you'll be able to tell which one it used.\n\n**Which parts of the colleague's note applied** (also in `ANSWER.md`):\n- **Used:** the `start`-not-`value` rename, sending targets as % of LTHR (the generator already did this), and their check-on-the-watch steps.\n- **Not used:** the warning about the top-level `description` text format. `gen.py` doesn't send a `description`, your readback shows the steps came through, and nobody reported steps missing in the Intervals web UI. If the